    "bottomLine": "The best edger for most yards: cordless, deep-cutting and quiet enough to use on a weekday evening, and the obvious buy if you already own EGO batteries.",
    "description": "The ME0800 is an 8-inch edger attachment on EGO's Multi-Head power head, and it is the cordless edger that comes closest to a gas stick edger in practice. The blade runs beside a guide wheel with a depth knob that EGO says goes down to 3 inches, which is deep enough to recut a border that has been left for a couple of seasons rather than just touching up a crisp one. The brushless power head has a variable-speed trigger plus a hi-low switch, so you can run it gently along a fragile paver edge and then open it up on a thick St. Augustine or Bermuda lip. The other reason it ranks first is the system around it. The same power head drives EGO's string trimmer, hedge and pole saw attachments, so one motor and one battery can cover most of the trimming jobs on a typical lot. The catch is cost and configuration. The ME0800 comes without a battery or charger. If you are new to EGO, buy the ME0801 kit, which bundles a 56V 2.5Ah battery that EGO rates for up to 35 minutes of run time, enough for the edges on most suburban lots in one pass."
  },
  {
    "id": "black-decker-le750-edger",
    "name": "BLACK+DECKER LE750 2-in-1 Edger and Trencher",
    "category": "12-Amp Corded Wheeled Edger",
    "badge": "Best Value",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 5000,
    "pros": [
      "12-amp motor and a 7.5-inch blade for a fraction of the cost of a battery kit",
      "Three depth settings: 1, 1-1/4 and 1-1/2 inches",
      "Converts to a trencher for bed borders, low-voltage lighting and pet-fence wire",
      "Wheeled chassis tracks the sidewalk edge without you holding the blade height",
      "More than 5,000 Amazon ratings, with the large majority at four or five stars"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Corded, so long driveways mean dragging 100 feet of heavy extension cord",
      "Maximum depth of 1-1/2 inches will not reclaim a badly overgrown border in one pass"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "If your edges are within reach of an outlet and you edge every few weeks, this does the job for very little money and doubles as a trencher.",
    "description": "The LE750 has been around for years, and it is still the edger we would point most budget buyers to. It is a wheeled corded machine with a 12-amp motor and a 7-1/2 inch blade, and Black+Decker gives it three blade depths of 1, 1-1/4 and 1-1/2 inches. That range is right for maintenance edging, where you are trimming the grass that has crept over a sidewalk since the last cut, but it is shallow for a border that has disappeared under runners. The 2-in-1 design is the reason to pick it over plain edgers in the same price class. Swing the blade over and it becomes a small trencher, useful for cutting a clean bed line, burying low-voltage landscape lighting cable or laying an invisible dog-fence wire. The trade-offs are the usual corded ones. You need a heavy-gauge extension cord, and on a long driveway the cord management is the most annoying part of the job. If your edging is a front walk and a short drive, that matters very little, and the price is hard to argue with."
  },
  {
    "id": "greenworks-40v-ed40l410-edger",
    "name": "Greenworks 40V 8-Inch Brushless Edger (ED40L410)",
    "category": "40V Battery Wheeled Edger Kit",
    "badge": "Best Cordless Kit",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 560,
    "pros": [
      "Kit includes a 40V 4.0Ah battery and charger, rated for up to 60 minutes",
      "Brushless motor with an 8-inch steel blade and guide wheel",
      "Tool-free depth adjustment, with a maximum cut depth of 2.25 inches per Greenworks",
      "Battery runs Greenworks' wider 40V range of more than 75 tools",
      "3-year tool and battery warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Far fewer Amazon ratings than the corded edgers, so long-term durability is less proven",
      "Shallower than the EGO at full depth"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cordless edger to buy if you are not already on a battery platform and want everything in one box for less than an EGO kit.",
    "description": "The ED40L410 is the cordless edger we would buy if you do not already own batteries from one brand. It comes as a complete kit with a 40V 4.0Ah battery and charger, and Greenworks rates it for up to 60 minutes of run time, which is more than enough for the edges on a typical lot and leaves battery left for a string trimmer on the same platform. The motor is brushless, the blade is an 8-inch steel blade riding next to a guide wheel, and depth adjusts without tools. Greenworks lists a maximum cut depth of 2.25 inches, which sits between the corded machines and the EGO, and is plenty for regular maintenance and for recutting a border that has drifted a little. Greenworks backs the tool and battery for three years. The honest caveats are that it has far fewer Amazon ratings than the corded edgers in this guide, so there is less evidence about how it holds up after several seasons, and it does not cut as deep as the EGO. For most homeowners starting from scratch, neither caveat outweighs the value of the complete kit."
  },
  {
    "id": "worx-wg896-edger",
    "name": "WORX WG896 12-Amp 7.5-Inch Edger and Trencher",
    "category": "12-Amp Corded Wheeled Edger",
    "badge": "Best Corded for Taller Users",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 15500,
    "pros": [
      "Adjustable-height shaft, which the fixed-handle budget edgers lack",
      "12-amp motor, 7.5-inch blade and a 4,700 rpm no-load speed",
      "Three depth settings from 1 to 1-1/2 inches, plus trenching",
      "Cutting-line indicator helps you hold a straight edge",
      "3-year warranty and more than 15,000 Amazon ratings"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Around 14 lbs, which you notice when lifting it over curbs and steps",
      "Same 1-1/2 inch depth limit and extension-cord hassle as other corded edgers"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Practically the LE750's twin, with an adjustable shaft and a longer warranty, so it is the better corded pick if you are tall or have a bad back.",
    "description": "On paper the WG896 and the Black+Decker LE750 are nearly the same tool. Both have 12-amp motors, 7-1/2 inch blades and three blade depths from 1 to 1-1/2 inches, and both convert to a trencher. Worx lists a no-load speed of 4,700 rpm and a weight of about 14 pounds. Two differences decide which one you should buy. The Worx has an adjustable shaft, so you can raise the handle rather than stooping over the tool for twenty minutes, which matters more than any spec if you are tall or have a sore back. It also carries a three-year warranty, longer than most budget corded edgers. The Worx adds a cutting-line indicator that helps you hold a straight line along a sidewalk joint, which is a small thing that makes a visible difference on the first few passes. With more than 15,000 Amazon ratings it is also one of the most widely used edgers sold. Everything that limits a corded edger applies here too. Depth tops out at 1-1/2 inches and you have to manage a cord. For a typical front walk and driveway, it is excellent value."
  },
  {
    "id": "mclane-101-4-75gt-7-edger",
    "name": "McLane 101-4.75GT-7 9-Inch Gas Walk-Behind Edger",
    "category": "Gas Walk-Behind Edger",
    "badge": "Best Walk-Behind",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.2,
    "reviewCount": 430,
    "pros": [
      "Briggs and Stratton engine with no cord and no battery to run out",
      "9-by-2-inch spring steel blade with adjustable depth control",
      "Single-lever blade clutch lets the engine idle while you reposition",
      "Four 7-inch rubber tires and a sealed-bearing cutter head",
      "Made in the USA by a company that has built walk-behind edgers for decades"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Heavy and bulky compared with any stick edger, and needs real storage space",
      "Gas engine maintenance, fuel stabilizer and noise come with it"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Worth it for long driveways, corner lots and anyone edging a lot of linear feet every week, and overkill for a front walk.",
    "description": "A walk-behind edger is a different class of machine from a handheld stick edger, and the McLane is the traditional version of it. It is a four-wheeled gas edger with a Briggs and Stratton engine, a 9-inch by 2-inch spring steel blade and adjustable depth control. A single lever engages the blade, so you can let the engine run while you roll the machine to the next section without the blade spinning. McLane says the cutter head runs on sealed precision ball bearings and that the blade can be set to cut vertically or horizontally. The point of a machine like this is that the chassis carries the weight and holds the blade angle for you. On a long driveway, a corner lot with a lot of sidewalk or a property with curbing on several sides, you walk at a steady pace and get the same edge every time, with nothing to hold up and no cord or battery to manage. The downsides are what you would expect. It is big and heavy, it needs fuel and engine care, and on a small lot it is a lot of machine to store for twenty minutes of work."
  },
  {
    "id": "earthquake-41273-edger",
    "name": "Earthquake 41273 79cc Walk-Behind Edger",
    "category": "Gas Walk-Behind Edger, Pivoting Blade",
    "badge": "Best for Overgrown Borders",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.5,
    "reviewCount": 140,
    "pros": [
      "79cc 4-cycle Viper engine, so there is no gas and oil mixing",
      "Ten depth positions, cutting up to 2.5 inches deep",
      "Blade pivots up to 20 degrees either way for beveled bed edges",
      "Tri-tip steel blade and four 8-inch airless wheels",
      "5-year limited warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "About 55 lbs and heavy to load into a car or lift over steps",
      "Few Amazon ratings so far, and Earthquake says it cannot ship to California"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The edger to choose when you are reclaiming lost borders or cutting beveled bed edges, not just tidying a sidewalk.",
    "description": "The Earthquake 41273 is the heavy option, and it earns its place for one job in particular: putting back edges that have disappeared. Its 79cc four-cycle Viper engine spins a tri-tip steel blade at 3,400 rpm, depth adjusts through ten positions down to 2.5 inches, and the blade pivots up to 20 degrees in either direction. That pivot is what separates it from the McLane and from every handheld here. A beveled cut along a mulch bed holds its shape better than a straight vertical one, and on a border that has grown over you can make the first pass angled and the second straight. The four airless 8-inch wheels mean there is no tire to go flat in the shed, and Earthquake backs it with a five-year limited warranty. It weighs about 55 pounds, so it is a two-person lift into a truck and awkward on steps. It also has far fewer Amazon ratings than the other picks, so treat its score with caution, and note that Earthquake lists it as not available to ship to California because of its engine."
  },
  {
    "id": "fiskars-long-handle-steel-edger",
    "name": "Fiskars 38.5-Inch Long-Handle Steel Edger",
    "category": "Manual Half-Moon Edger",
    "badge": "Best Manual Edger",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.5,
    "reviewCount": 1500,
    "pros": [
      "Welded 14-gauge hardened steel blade and powder-coated 18-gauge steel shaft",
      "T-handle for a two-handed grip and an extra-large foot platform",
      "No fuel, no battery, no cord and almost nothing to maintain",
      "Lifetime warranty from Fiskars",
      "Cuts bed edges and curves that wheeled edgers handle poorly"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Hard physical work on long straight runs of sidewalk",
      "Slow for maintenance edging compared with any powered edger"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right tool for cutting bed lines and the occasional border, and a useful partner to a powered edger even if you own one.",
    "description": "A half-moon edger is the oldest tool in this guide and still one of the most useful. The Fiskars has a welded 14-gauge hardened steel blade, an 18-gauge powder-coated steel shaft and a T-handle, so you can drive it with both hands and your full weight on the oversized foot platform. That combination is what makes it work: a sharp blade pressed straight down cuts turf roots cleanly, where a flimsy edger bends and tears. It is the best tool here for cutting new bed lines, following curves around trees and borders, and making the deep initial cut that a powered edger can then maintain. It needs no fuel, cord or battery, it hangs on a nail, and Fiskars backs it with a lifetime warranty. Where it falls down is long straight runs of hardscape. Edging 150 feet of sidewalk and driveway by hand every few weeks is hard, slow work, and most people who try it give up and buy a powered edger. Think of it as a bed-edging tool first and a sidewalk tool only for small properties."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Lawn Edger",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-lawn-edger/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need a lawn edger if I have a string trimmer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not always. Turning a trimmer head sideways works for short edges you maintain every week or two. A dedicated edger cuts deeper, leaves a cleaner vertical face, throws less grit and saves a lot of trimmer line, so it is worth buying once you have more than about 100 feet of edging or warm-season grass that creeps fast."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How deep should I edge my lawn?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For routine maintenance along a sidewalk or driveway, about an inch is enough. For an overgrown border, or grasses like Bermuda and St. Augustine that spread by runners, cut 1-1/2 to 2 inches to sever the stolons. Avoid going deeper than you need, because it wears blades and risks buried cables and irrigation."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a battery edger powerful enough?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most homeowners, yes. Brushless battery edgers like the EGO and Greenworks cut cleanly through typical edges and can go deeper than corded wheeled models. Where they struggle is long sessions on badly overgrown borders, where battery life and motor load both become limits. That is the job for a gas walk-behind."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often should I edge?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "During the growing season, every two to four weeks keeps edges crisp with a quick shallow pass. Warm-season lawns in hot weather may need it every two weeks. Leaving it for two months means a slow, deep recut, so a regular schedule is actually less work overall. Cool-season lawns can usually go a little longer between cuts."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a lawn edger cut a new flower bed edge?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, and the trenching mode on edgers like the Black+Decker LE750 and Worx WG896 is designed for it. For curves, a manual half-moon edger is easier to control. Lay a hose along the line you want, cut it by hand or with the edger, and maintain it with the powered edger afterward."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between an edger and a trencher?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "An edger cuts a narrow vertical slit along a hard edge to stop grass creeping. A trencher cuts a slightly wider, deeper slot for bed borders or for burying low-voltage cable. Several corded edgers convert between the two by changing the blade position, which makes them useful for small landscaping jobs."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long do edger blades last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on how much concrete the blade touches and how often you edge, but most homeowners replace a blade every one or two seasons. Replace it when the corners have rounded off or the edge starts looking torn rather than sliced. Always buy the blade size and arbor that match your model."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Lawn & Yard Care",
    tags: [
      "best lawn edger",
      "lawn edger",
      "battery lawn edger",
      "electric lawn edger",
      "walk behind edger",
      "manual lawn edger"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated October 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Lawn Edgers</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          From a cordless stick edger to a 79cc walk-behind and a steel half-moon, matched to the length of your edges and how overgrown they are.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#ego-me0800-edger"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Jump to the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="11.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#edger-types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Types of Edger Compared</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#edger-vs-trimmer" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Edger vs String Trimmer</a>
          <a href="#depth-blades" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Blade Depth and Wear</a>
          <a href="#power-sources" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Battery vs Corded vs Gas</a>
          <a href="#how-to-edge" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. How to Cut a Clean Edge</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Edger Safety</a>
          <a href="#maintenance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Blade Changes and Upkeep</a>
          <a href="#who-should-skip" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Who Should Skip One</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most people buying an edger need less machine than they think. A battery stick edger or a corded wheeled edger will keep a sidewalk and driveway crisp in minutes. The heavy gas walk-behinds are for long driveways and borders that have disappeared, and a cheap half-moon edger belongs in every shed for bed lines.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            A lawn edger has one job, which is to cut a clean vertical line between grass and something that is not grass: a sidewalk, a driveway, a curb or a planting bed. Any tool that spins a blade or a string can do that badly. The difference between a good edger and a frustrating one is whether it holds the line and the depth without you fighting it, and whether it is still pleasant to use on the fifteenth week of the season.
          </p>

          <p>
            We started from the types of edger actually sold to homeowners: battery stick edgers, corded wheeled edgers, gas walk-behind edgers and manual half-moon edgers. For each type we looked for models with long histories and large numbers of owner ratings, then checked the manufacturer specifications that matter for edging, mainly blade size, maximum depth, how depth is adjusted and how the tool is powered. We have not run these edgers through a lab test, and we do not pretend to have. The picks are based on published specifications, the weight of owner feedback and how each design suits a particular kind of yard.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prices move constantly, so we describe value in tiers rather than dollar figures. Where a spec could not be confirmed from the manufacturer or a major retailer listing, we left it out. Ratings are Amazon star averages and rating counts, rounded down. A product with tens of thousands of ratings and a 4.4 average tells you more about real-world reliability than a newer model with fifty ratings and a 4.8, and we weighed them accordingly.
          </p>

          <p>
            One thing we did not reward is raw power. A 79cc engine is impressive and pointless on a townhouse front walk. The right edger is the one that matches the length of your edges, how overgrown they are and how often you will actually use it, which is why this list runs from a 2-pound hand tool to a 55-pound walk-behind.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="edger-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Stick, Wheeled, Walk-Behind or Manual
          </h2>

          <p>
            Stick edgers look like a string trimmer with a vertical blade where the spool would be. You carry the weight, a small guide wheel rides on the pavement, and the blade cuts beside it. Gas stick edgers are what professional crews use because they are light enough to carry all day and fast enough to edge a house in a few minutes. Battery versions like the EGO now get close to them for homeowners.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wheeled electric edgers, like the Black+Decker LE750 and Worx WG896, put the motor and blade on a small two- or three-wheeled chassis. The chassis carries the weight and sets the blade height, so they are easy to control even if you have never edged before. Most are corded and stop at about 1-1/2 inches of depth, which is right for maintenance and shallow for reclaiming lost borders.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas walk-behind edgers are the heavy end. They roll on four wheels, carry a small engine and a 9-inch blade, and they will run all afternoon without a recharge. They make sense when you have a long driveway, a lot of sidewalk or curbing, or edges that have been neglected. Manual half-moon edgers sit at the other extreme: a sharpened steel blade on a long handle that you drive in with your foot.
          </p>

