        <div id="edger-vs-trimmer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Edger vs String Trimmer Edging
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plenty of people never buy an edger at all. They rotate the head of their string trimmer 90 degrees and run the line along the sidewalk. It works, and if you already own a good trimmer it is the free option. Many trimmers, including several in our <a href="/best-string-trimmer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best string trimmer guide</a>, have a rotating head or an edging wheel for exactly this reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            The difference is in the result and the wear. A string throws grit, chews the edge rather than slicing it, and wears line quickly against concrete, so you will be reloading the spool far more often. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-restring-a-weed-eater/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">how to restring a weed eater</a> makes that less painful, but it is still the main running cost of trimmer edging. A steel blade cuts a crisp vertical face, goes deeper and throws less debris across the driveway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Depth is the other gap. A trimmer cuts at the surface and maybe a little below. That keeps a sharp edge looking neat for a week, but runners keep growing under the slab line and the grass creeps back faster. An edger cutting an inch or more down severs those runners, so a well-cut edge holds its shape longer between passes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our rule of thumb is simple. If you edge less than about 100 linear feet and do it every week or two, your trimmer is probably enough. If you have more than that, warm-season grasses that spread by runners, or edges you want to look professionally cut, a dedicated edger pays for itself in time and line. If you are shopping for the trimmer side of this, the <a href="/best-weed-eater/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best weed eater roundup</a> covers which models edge best.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="depth-blades" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Blade Depth, Blade Size and Blade Wear
          </h2>

          <p>
            Edger blades are usually 7-1/2 to 9 inches across, and the blade size matters less than the maximum depth the machine allows. For routine maintenance along a clean sidewalk, about an inch is plenty. For a border that has been ignored for a season, or for grass like Bermuda, zoysia and St. Augustine that spreads by stolons over the concrete, you want 1-1/2 to 2 inches available so you can cut below the runners.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is where the categories separate. The corded wheeled edgers in this guide stop at 1-1/2 inches. Greenworks lists 2.25 inches for the ED40L410, the Earthquake goes to 2.5 inches and EGO quotes 3 inches for its edger attachment. Deeper is not always better. Cutting deeper than you need wears blades faster, throws more soil and risks cable or irrigation lines buried close to the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Blades are a consumable. An edger blade grinds against concrete on every pass and will round off over a season or two, after which it tears grass rather than cutting it. Most replacement blades are inexpensive, and you can buy them from the manufacturer or third-party suppliers, but check the arbor size and blade length match your model before ordering. A dull blade is the most common reason an edger that used to cut cleanly starts leaving a ragged line.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pay attention to how depth is set. Tool-free knobs and levers, like those on the EGO and Greenworks, encourage you to adjust depth to suit each edge. Fixed notches that need a wrench tend to get set once and left, which usually means cutting too deep on a sharp edge and too shallow on an overgrown one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-sources" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Battery vs Corded vs Gas
          </h2>

          <p>
            Battery edgers are the best fit for most homeowners now. They start instantly, run quietly enough for an evening, need no fuel and have no cord to drag across the lawn. The real cost is the battery. If you already own batteries from EGO, Greenworks, Ryobi or another platform, a bare edger is a cheap add-on. If you do not, the kit price is high, and you should think about which platform you want to commit to. Our <a href="/cordless-tool-batteries-explained/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">cordless tool batteries explainer</a> covers how to choose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Corded edgers are the value choice and are genuinely good tools. A 12-amp motor has plenty of power for a 1-1/2 inch cut, they never run out of charge and there is nothing to maintain beyond the blade. The drawback is the cord. A 12-amp tool needs a heavy-gauge outdoor extension cord, and on a long driveway you spend a lot of the job flipping the cord out of the way.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas makes sense in two cases: walk-behind machines for large properties and heavily overgrown edges, and stick edgers for professionals. A gas stick edger like the Echo PE-2620, which Echo specs with a 25.4cc two-stroke engine, a 2:1 gear reduction and a curved shaft, is a superb tool, but it is sold mainly through dealers and is more machine, noise and maintenance than most homeowners need. Four-stroke walk-behinds avoid fuel mixing, but still need oil changes and stabilized fuel over winter.
          </p>

          <p>
            A practical way to decide: if you edge for less than 30 minutes at a time and own batteries, buy battery. If you do not own batteries and your edges are near an outlet, buy corded. If you have hundreds of feet of edging, or edges that have vanished, look at a walk-behind. Edging is also the job where a battery platform pays off fastest, because the same battery runs your trimmer, <a href="/best-hedge-trimmer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">hedge trimmer</a> and blower.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-edge" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Cut a Clean Edge the First Time
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start by finding the line. On a sidewalk or driveway, that is the edge of the concrete, and the guide wheel on most edgers rides on the pavement to keep the blade tight against it. On a bed edge, mark the line first with a garden hose laid in the curve you want or a string line for straight runs. Cutting freehand by eye is how bed edges end up wavy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Set the depth shallower than you think on the first pass. If the edge has grown over, make one shallow pass to establish the line, then a second at full depth. Trying to plough through 2 inches of matted turf in one go bogs the motor, throws the blade off line and leaves a ragged edge that takes weeks to grow back neatly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Walk at a steady, slow pace and let the blade do the work. Pushing an edger fast makes it skip and wander. Keep the guide wheel on the hard surface and the blade guard positioned so clippings land on the pavement, where they are easy to blow off, rather than in the lawn or a flower bed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean up immediately. Edging leaves strips of turf and soil on the concrete, and if it sits it stains, tracks inside and blows back into the lawn. A blower takes seconds. On a driveway with weeds pushing through joints as well as at the edges, our guide to <a href="/how-to-kill-weeds-in-driveway-cracks/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">killing weeds in driveway cracks</a> covers the part an edger cannot reach.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then keep it up. A clean edge recut every two to four weeks in the growing season takes a few minutes. Letting it go for two months means starting over with a deep, slow cut, which is exactly the job that makes people think they need a more powerful edger than they really do.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Edger Safety: Sparks, Rocks and Buried Lines
          </h2>

          <p>
            An edger blade spinning against concrete throws grit, small stones and, when it touches the slab, sparks. Wear safety glasses every time, long trousers and closed shoes. Keep children and pets well away, and be aware of cars parked along the driveway, because a thrown stone can chip paint or crack glass.
          </p>

          <p>
            Know what is buried along your edges before you cut deep. Low-voltage landscape lighting cable, invisible dog-fence wire and drip irrigation are often laid just under the turf along walks and beds. A blade set to 2 or 3 inches will sever them instantly. Run the first pass shallow and check, or mark known runs with flags.
          </p>

          <p>
            Respect the blade when clearing a jam. Turf and roots wrap around edger blades, especially on overgrown borders. Stop the engine or motor, remove the battery or unplug the cord, and then clear it. On gas walk-behinds, disengage the blade clutch and shut the engine off. Most edger injuries happen to people clearing a blade that they assumed had stopped.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hearing protection is sensible with any gas edger and on long sessions with corded or battery tools. Walk-behind gas machines are loud enough that neighbors will notice, so the courtesy rules for any power equipment apply: not early in the morning, and not for an hour at a time on a weekend afternoon.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Blade Changes and Upkeep
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single most useful maintenance habit is checking the blade. Look at it every few uses. When the corners have worn round or the blade has shortened noticeably, replace it. Sharpening an edger blade is possible, but they are cheap enough and worn unevenly enough that replacement is usually the better use of time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Change the blade with the power removed, wear gloves and note the order of washers before you take it apart. Some edgers use a reverse-threaded blade bolt, and most have washers or spacers that must go back in the right sequence, so check the manual before you start. Tighten to the manual's figure rather than as tight as you can, because overtightening can damage the shaft or bearings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean the blade guard after each use. Wet clippings and soil pack into the guard and around the guide wheel, which drags the motor and holds moisture against metal. A stiff brush when the debris is dry is enough. On battery tools, store the battery indoors over winter at a partial charge, as the manufacturer recommends.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas edgers need the usual engine care: fresh fuel with stabilizer, clean air filters, and an oil change on four-stroke walk-behinds at the interval in the manual. Run the tank dry or stabilize the fuel before winter storage, because varnish in a small carburetor is the most common reason an edger will not start in spring.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Skip a Dedicated Edger
          </h2>

          <p>
            If your property has a short front walk and no driveway edge to speak of, an edger is a tool that will hang on the wall for 364 days a year. A string trimmer turned on its side and a half-moon edger for the beds will give you a result almost as good for a fraction of the cost and storage space.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your hardscape has a mowing strip, metal or plastic edging, or a raised curb that the mower deck already trims against, you may not need to edge at all. Edging is mostly about stopping grass from creeping over flat concrete, and a physical barrier does that permanently.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your grass is a cool-season type like fescue or Kentucky bluegrass, it creeps more slowly over edges than warm-season grasses that spread by runners. A cut every month or so may be enough, and a trimmer handles that comfortably. Warm-season lawns in the South are where a proper edger earns its keep.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if you are tempted by a gas walk-behind because it sounds more serious, be honest about your lot. Unless you have a lot of linear feet or edges that have been lost, a battery or corded edger will do the job with less noise, less weight and far less storage. Spend the difference on a leaf cleanup tool from our <a href="/best-leaf-vacuum/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">leaf vacuum guide</a> instead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying more edger than the yard needs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 79cc walk-behind on a 40-foot front walk is noise, weight and storage for no benefit. Measure your linear feet of edging first. Under about 150 feet, a battery or corded edger does the job well, and a string trimmer may be enough if you edge often.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting full depth on an overgrown border in one pass</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Forcing the blade 2 inches into matted turf bogs the motor, throws the edger off line and leaves a ragged cut. Make a shallow pass to establish the line, then a second deeper pass. The result is straighter, faster and easier on the tool and your back.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring what is buried along the edge</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Landscape lighting cable, dog-fence wire and drip lines often run just under the turf beside walks and beds. A blade set deep will cut them in an instant. Flag known runs, start with a shallow pass, and check the cut before you set the edger to its maximum depth.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a dull, rounded blade</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Edger blades grind against concrete every pass and wear round within a season or two. A worn blade tears grass instead of slicing it and leaves a fuzzy edge that looks worse within days. Replacement blades are cheap, so check the blade every few uses and swap it out.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a light extension cord</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 12-amp corded edger on a long, thin indoor cord loses power, runs hot and can trip breakers. Use a heavy-gauge outdoor-rated cord sized for the length you need, and route it behind you so the blade never crosses it while you work along the driveway.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a lawn edger if I have a string trimmer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not always. Turning a trimmer head sideways works for short edges you maintain every week or two. A dedicated edger cuts deeper, leaves a cleaner vertical face, throws less grit and saves a lot of trimmer line, so it is worth buying once you have more than about 100 feet of edging or warm-season grass that creeps fast.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How deep should I edge my lawn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For routine maintenance along a sidewalk or driveway, about an inch is enough. For an overgrown border, or grasses like Bermuda and St. Augustine that spread by runners, cut 1-1/2 to 2 inches to sever the stolons. Avoid going deeper than you need, because it wears blades and risks buried cables and irrigation.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a battery edger powerful enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homeowners, yes. Brushless battery edgers like the EGO and Greenworks cut cleanly through typical edges and can go deeper than corded wheeled models. Where they struggle is long sessions on badly overgrown borders, where battery life and motor load both become limits. That is the job for a gas walk-behind.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I edge?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                During the growing season, every two to four weeks keeps edges crisp with a quick shallow pass. Warm-season lawns in hot weather may need it every two weeks. Leaving it for two months means a slow, deep recut, so a regular schedule is actually less work overall. Cool-season lawns can usually go a little longer between cuts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a lawn edger cut a new flower bed edge?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and the trenching mode on edgers like the Black+Decker LE750 and Worx WG896 is designed for it. For curves, a manual half-moon edger is easier to control. Lay a hose along the line you want, cut it by hand or with the edger, and maintain it with the powered edger afterward.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between an edger and a trencher?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An edger cuts a narrow vertical slit along a hard edge to stop grass creeping. A trencher cuts a slightly wider, deeper slot for bed borders or for burying low-voltage cable. Several corded edgers convert between the two by changing the blade position, which makes them useful for small landscaping jobs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do edger blades last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on how much concrete the blade touches and how often you edge, but most homeowners replace a blade every one or two seasons. Replace it when the corners have rounded off or the edge starts looking torn rather than sliced. Always buy the blade size and arbor that match your model.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
